 For homogeneous zero-block deterministic thresholds \(\{c_0,c_1\}\), the finite-grid/limit calculation gives \(\kappa=\alpha(c_0,c_1)\). To transfer this gap to regret, one must add: same prefix action law across candidates, candidates interpreted as conditional expected payoff laws rather than realized hidden futures, a cap \(B_u\) on outside/post-divergence learner surplus, and probability weighting for rare prefix events. Small \(\kappa\) is not by itself a learnability theorem.
+
+**CL-023: Sharp finite-\(m\) multi-threshold constant.**  
+Status: plausible construction; upper bound not yet proved.  
+For the supremum over choices of \(m\) terminal thresholds with top threshold normalized to \(1\), the candidate sharp local gap is
+\[
+\kappa_m^*=\left(\frac{m-1}{m}\right)^m.
+\]
+The construction uses \(r=(m-1)/m\), thresholds \(c_i=r^{m-i}\), and equal-revenue tails \(\Pr(C\ge c_i)=r^{i-1}\). This recovers \(1/4\) at \(m=2\) and approaches \(1/e\). It is false if read as the value for every fixed \(m\)-point threshold set; e.g. \(\{0.9,1\}\) has two-threshold gap \(0.09\). A rigorous upper proof for support size \(m\) is still needed.
+
+**CL-024: Removing the \(O(1)\) intermediate-round loss in same-path blocks.**  
+Status: plausible but not promoted.  
+For same-path blocks \(0^N,c_0,c_1\), reports suggest the \(O(1)\) term in CL-018 can be removed in a `sup`-comparator proof because the longer-horizon comparator's near-\(c_1\) intermediate quote cancels the learner's possible intermediate reward bounded by \(c_1\). This needs a complete strict-crossing proof; \(\eta\)-comparator proofs may still lose \(O(\eta N)\) terms.
